{
  "dom_id": "step-q97751",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number of calls a call center receives in an hour.\n\nII. The number obtained when picking an integer number between $-5$ and $5$ at random.\n\nIII. The amount of time, in minutes, between successive calls received by a call center.",
  "worked_solution": "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the number of calls can be any positive integer, so the support is not finite. Moreover, each number of calls is not equally likely.\n\n- Random variable II can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are a finite number of integers between $-5$ and $5,$ and since the selection is made at random, each integer has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the amount of time between events is a real number, so the support is not finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
